WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.120
"Use Fiber to Burn Calories in Your Sleep
with an Intestinal Workout"

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:10.320
Fiber can literally help
flush calories away,

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.560
assisting with
weight management.

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:13.680
A review entitled

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.000
"Food Fibre as an Obstacle
to Energy Intake"

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.360
summarized the four Ds

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:21.920
by which dietary fiber results
in reduced caloric intake.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:25.680
There's the dilution of calories
by expanding the volume of food,

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:28.720
distention of the stomach
through fluid absorption,

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:31.520
delay in stomach emptying
of the gelled mass,

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:33.120
and dumping of calories

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:37.040
by blocking the absorption
of other macronutrients.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.840
That fourth D triggers
a fifth phenomenon,

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:42.080
known as the ileal brake.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:43.520
As Dr. Greger has covered,

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:45.840
the ileum is the last part
of the small intestine

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.840
before it empties
into the colon.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:51.040
When undigested nutrients
are detected that far down,

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:53.360
the body puts the brakes
on further eating

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:55.760
by curbing our appetite.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:56.960
So, there are lots of ways

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:00.000
eating more fiber can help
with weight regulation.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.240
Across more
than 10 weight-loss studies,

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:04.720
those randomized to consume
higher-fiber diets

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:06.000
lost more weight,

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.200
even when calorie intake
was fixed.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:10.480
So, more weight loss,

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:14.640
even when prescribed
the same number of calories.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:18.080
If it wasn't the "calories in"
side of the equation,

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:20.720
was it the "calories out"?

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:23.120
Normally that means things
like exercise,

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:25.440
but in the case
of high-fiber diets,

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:28.320
there literally
are calories out,

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.920
as in out the other end
and flushed down the toilet.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:34.160
But the same-calorie,
higher-fiber groups

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:37.200
were losing more weight
than could be accounted for,

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.280
even after taking into account
the excess calorie dumping.

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:43.680
Where were the calories going?

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:45.920
To solve the mystery
of the missing calories,

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:48.720
researchers fed people
different amounts of fiber

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:50.720
and sealed them
in an airtight chamber

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:52.640
called a whole-body calorimeter

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:56.160
to closely monitor
their metabolic rate.

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:58.080
Those with more fiber
in their system

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:01.440
were burning more calories
even in their sleep!

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.920
Only about 2% more,
but that translated

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:06.720
into burning
almost 50 more calories a day

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:09.440
without getting off the couch.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:11.920
For people on long-term
fiber-rich diets,

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:13.440
they figured that all that fiber

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:16.160
might bulk up
their intestinal lining,

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:20.240
which is a highly
metabolically-active tissue.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:23.120
Why were they spontaneously
burning off more energy

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:25.840
even when they were asleep?

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:27.840
It turns out
all that extra fiber

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:30.560
may have been giving their gut
a workout.

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:32.880
Our intestines
are muscular tubes.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:36.400
So, our small intestine is like
20 feet (6.10 m) of muscle,

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:39.680
which contracts in waves
to move food along.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:43.680
But fiber-depleted foods
don't offer much resistance.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:45.280
The apple juice
and Wonder Bread

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:47.120
get absorbed almost immediately

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:49.520
and don't put up
much resistance.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:50.800
It's like weight lifting

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:53.520
with a barbell
made out of Styrofoam.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:55.280
Not very effective.

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:58.000
Researchers had people swallow
long strings of electrodes

00:02:58.000 --> 00:02:59.520
to measure
the electrical activity

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:02.320
of the muscular contractions
of the intestines

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:06.720
of those eating low-fiber meals
versus high-fiber meals.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:08.720
The individuals
eating more fiber

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:12.640
not only had stronger, faster,
longer contractions,

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:15.920
but reduced the periods
of intestinal inactivity.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:18.640
Turns out our gut
can be sedentary, too.

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:20.560
But if we eat lots
of fiber-rich foods,

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:21.760
we can be asleep

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:27.692
and our gut will still
be exercising all night long.

